findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

Removes the `ALLOWED` origin check and always returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` set to the caller-supplied `origin` (or `*`) together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** a malicious page at `https://evil.example` sends a credentialed XHR/fetch to this API. The browser attaches the victim's cookies; the response includes `ACAO: https://evil.example` and `ACAC: true`, so the attacker can read authenticated API responses (account data, tokens in body, etc.).

Native mobile HTTP stacks do not enforce CORS, so this does not unlock them. Webviews still need an explicit allowlist of known origins — restore something like:

```ts
const ALLOWED = new Set([
  "https://app.example.com",
  // add known webview origins only
]);

export function corsHeaders(origin: string | null): Record<string, string> {
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
}
```

Also: `ACAO: *` + `ACAC: true` (the `origin ?? "*"` branch) is invalid per the CORS spec and browsers will reject it, so null-Origin clients still fail while every real browser Origin is trusted.
